add('para', '**Abstract:** ' + (
    'Machine-learning maps of livestock disease are usually checked with random cross-validation. '
    'We studied household-reported contagious bovine pleuropneumonia (CBPP), foot-and-mouth disease (FMD) and lumpy skin disease (LSD) in 3,340 Ethiopian households at 462 locations from five survey campaigns (2012-2019). '
    'Under 81 km spatial block cross-validation, we compared nested feature sets, from five environmental variables (FS1) to 39 features (FS5) that add climate indices, coordinates, neighbourhood and cross-disease rates and sampling density, using ExtraTrees and a 23-model rank-average ensemble. '
    'Neighbourhood rates used training labels only. '
    f'Random cross-validation gave mean ensemble ROC-AUC {min(gap_a.values()):.2f}-{max(gap_a.values()):.2f} higher than spatial validation, mainly because {same_loc_random:.0f}% of its test households had training households at the same location. '
    f'Under spatial validation, FS5 raised mean FMD ROC-AUC from {fm("FMD", "FS1"):.3f} to {fm("FMD", "FS5"):.3f}. '
    f'The ablation showed that this gain came mainly from climate indices ({inc_m[("FS2 - FS1", "FMD")]:+.3f}) and coordinates ({inc_m[("FS3 - FS2", "FMD")]:+.3f}), while neighbourhood and cross-disease rates added little once location was known ({inc_m[("FS4 - FS3", "FMD")]:+.3f}) and partly encoded the survey campaign. The leakage audit showed that these rates are safe, not that they are needed. '
    f'The FMD gain exceeded all label permutations, but the Holm-adjusted permutation p-value was {holm_fmd:.3f}. FS5 gave no consistent gain for CBPP or LSD, and the LSD probabilities had no skill beyond the prevalence. '
    f'Survey year explained {100 * eta_year.min():.1f}-{100 * eta_year.max():.1f}% of outcome variance and could be predicted from the features; within single years ROC-AUC fell to {min(wy_best.values()):.2f}-{max(wy_best.values()):.2f}, and models trained on 2012-2015 showed no useful discrimination on 2018-2019 (ROC-AUC {min(temp_vals):.2f}-{max(temp_vals):.2f}). '
    'Such models suit retrospective mapping within the surveyed period, not forecasting.'))
add('para', '**Keywords:** Spatial cross-validation, Survey-campaign confounding, Data leakage, Feature ablation, Explainable machine learning, Area of applicability, Cattle disease mapping.')

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1 introduction
add('h1', '1. Introduction')
para('Contagious bovine pleuropneumonia (CBPP), foot-and-mouth disease (FMD) and lumpy skin disease (LSD) are among the most important cattle diseases in Ethiopia. Livestock keepers rank them among their main health problems {Gizaw2020}, district outbreak records show that FMD is widespread {Jemberu2016}, and clinical LSD is common in many areas {Gari2010}. Control programmes need to know where these diseases are reported, but surveillance data are sparse and come from separate projects at different times.')
para('Machine-learning models can fill gaps between surveyed locations. Some studies report high accuracy for predicting livestock disease occurrence from climate and location data {AfshariSafavi2022}. Such results are usually based on random splits of the data. When nearby observations are similar, random splits put near-copies of the test data into the training set, and accuracy is overstated {Roberts2017, Ploton2020}. Spatial block cross-validation reduces this problem by holding out whole areas {Roberts2017, Valavi2019}.')
para('Spatial validation does not remove every source of optimism. First, engineered neighbourhood features, such as disease rates in nearby locations, are useful predictors {Hengl2018}, but they are built from outcome labels and can leak test information if they are constructed carelessly {Kaufman2011, Kapoor2023}. A feature that passes a leakage check is safe to use, but this does not show that it is needed. Second, survey data are often collected in campaigns. Each campaign covers different places in a different year, and reported prevalence may change from one campaign to the next. A model can then score well by learning which campaign an area belonged to, rather than which households within an area are at higher risk. This is a form of dataset shift and sampling bias {MorenoTorres2012, Phillips2009}, and it is rarely measured in livestock disease mapping.')
para('It is also important to separate two tasks. Retrospective mapping estimates where disease was reported during the period that the surveys cover, including places between surveyed locations. Forecasting predicts where disease will be reported in a later period. A model can do the first reasonably well and still fail at the second, because the later period may have different areas, prevalence levels and reporting conditions.')
para('We use a public ILRI dataset of household-reported CBPP, FMD and LSD in Ethiopia {Gizaw2026data}. A recent preprint analysed the same surveys with generalised additive mixed models and kriging, and modelled survey year and location jointly through a year-location factor {Gizaw2026preprint}. We address the machine-learning side of this question. The study makes four contributions:')
for t in ['**Spatial generalisation.** We compare random and spatial block cross-validation, run with the same code, for four models and three diseases, and we describe the distance between test and training data, the area of applicability and the sensitivity to proximity and block size.',
          '**A leakage-safe framework for outcome-derived neighbourhood features.** We construct neighbourhood and cross-disease rates from training labels only, with the block of the query location excluded, and test the construction with an independent reimplementation, label-flipping checks and permutation controls that rebuild the whole pipeline. A feature ablation (FS1 to FS5) then shows what each feature group adds, and SHAP explanations show how the models use the features.',
          '**Survey-campaign confounding.** We measure how much of the pooled spatial accuracy comes from differences between survey campaigns, using within-year discrimination, survey-year identification from the features, and models trained on one campaign at a time.',
          '**Retrospective mapping versus temporal generalisation.** We test directly whether models trained on earlier campaigns transfer to later ones.']:
    bullet(t)
para('Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 describes the data, features, models, validation and statistics. Section 4 presents and discusses the results, Section 5 lists the limitations and Section 6 concludes.')

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 related work
add('h1', '2. Related work')
add('h2', '2.1 Spatial validation')
para('Roberts et al. showed that ignoring spatial, temporal or hierarchical structure during cross-validation underestimates prediction error, and recommended blocking {Roberts2017}. Valavi et al. provided tools to build spatial blocks whose size follows the range of spatial autocorrelation {Valavi2019}. Ploton et al. found that maps of forest biomass with good random-validation scores had almost no predictive power under spatial validation {Ploton2020}.')
para('Not all authors agree that spatial cross-validation is the right target. Wadoux et al. argued that map accuracy should be estimated by design-based inference from probability samples, and that spatial cross-validation can be pessimistic {Wadoux2021}. De Bruin et al. proposed weighting schemes for clustered samples {deBruin2022}. Milà et al. and Linnenbrink et al. matched the distances between test and training points to those between prediction and training points {Mila2022, Linnenbrink2024}. Meyer and Pebesma proposed the area of applicability to flag predictions in unfamiliar predictor conditions {Meyer2021}. Our task is to predict areas without survey data, so we use spatial blocks, and we report distances, the area of applicability, proximity buffers and block-size sensitivity because the right block size is not known in advance.')
add('h2', '2.2 Machine learning in livestock disease prediction')
para('Studies of CBPP, FMD and LSD in Ethiopia have mostly used questionnaires, outbreak reports and regression models to describe risk factors {Gizaw2020, Jemberu2016, Gari2010}. Tesfaye et al. analysed space-time patterns of reported LSD outbreaks {Tesfaye2024}. Afshari Safavi compared machine-learning methods for predicting LSD occurrence from meteorological and geospatial features and reported high accuracy with randomly split data {AfshariSafavi2022}. Gizaw et al. mapped CBPP, FMD and LSD risk from the household surveys used here {Gizaw2026preprint}. To our knowledge, no study has evaluated machine-learning models on these data with spatial block validation and an explicit survey-campaign analysis.')
add('h2', '2.3 Feature engineering, leakage and explainability')
para('Distances to observations, and summaries of nearby outcomes, are common predictors in spatial random forests {Hengl2018}. Such features are a form of target encoding, so they must be built from training labels only. Kaufman et al. defined leakage as information about the target that would not be available at prediction time {Kaufman2011}. Kapoor and Narayanan found leakage in hundreds of machine-learning studies across many fields {Kapoor2023}. Model selection can also leak optimism: choosing a model or setting by a noisy cross-validation score over-fits the selection itself {Cawley2010}.')
para('SHAP values attribute a prediction to the input features using Shapley values from game theory {Lundberg2017}, and exact values can be computed efficiently for tree ensembles {Lundberg2020}. Partial dependence plots show how the average prediction changes with one feature {Friedman2001}. Both describe what a fitted model uses. When features are correlated, as coordinates and neighbourhood rates are, a model can rely on one feature that another could replace, so explanations should be read together with an ablation that refits the model without each group.')
add('h2', '2.4 Survey-campaign confounding')
para('When data are pooled from surveys run at different times and places, the training and test populations can differ in their predictors, in their prevalence, or in both. Moreno-Torres et al. describe these as covariate shift, prior-probability shift and concept shift {MorenoTorres2012}. Phillips et al. showed that biased sampling distorts species distribution models unless the background sample shares the same bias {Phillips2009}. Yates et al. found that predictive models often transfer poorly to new places and times {Yates2018}. For the Ethiopian surveys, Gizaw et al. included a year-location factor because survey year and location could not be separated {Gizaw2026preprint}. We measure how much this confounding affects machine-learning accuracy.')

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 methods
add('h1', '3. Materials and methods')
add('h2', '3.1 Dataset description')
para(f'The data come from household questionnaire surveys conducted by five livestock projects in 2012, 2014, 2015, 2018 and 2019, published by ILRI under a CC BY 4.0 licence {{Gizaw2026data, Gizaw2026preprint}}. Each household reported whether CBPP, FMD and LSD occurred in its cattle (binary outcomes). The outcomes are household-reported occurrence, not laboratory-confirmed infection. The data contain 3,340 households at 462 locations, and 449 locations were surveyed in a single year. Overall reported prevalence was {prev["CBPP"]:.1f}% for CBPP, {prev["FMD"]:.1f}% for FMD and {prev["LSD"]:.1f}% for LSD ({T("data")}). The campaigns covered different parts of the country, and reported prevalence changed strongly between them ({F("years")}).')
