add('h2', '3.2 Data preprocessing')
para('The data come as three files, one per disease, with the same households. We checked that household IDs, environmental variables, coordinates and the year-region code were identical in all three files and merged them into one record per household with three outcomes. Survey year and region were read from the year-region code. Locations are unique coordinate pairs, recorded to 0.1 degree. Coordinates were converted to kilometres with an equirectangular projection centred on the mean latitude, for blocks and distances.')
para(f'There were no missing values and no duplicate households, so no imputation was needed. Tree models used raw values, and linear, distance-based and kernel models standardised their inputs inside each training set. The files also contain a variable named Weight that closely follows the share of households at the same location with the same outcome (Spearman correlation {WL["spearman_Weight_vs_same_outcome_share"].min():.2f}-{WL["spearman_Weight_vs_same_outcome_share"].max():.2f}). On its own it separates cases from non-cases with ROC-AUC of {WL.loc["LSD", "discrimination_max(AUC,1-AUC)"]:.2f}-{WL.loc["FMD", "discrimination_max(AUC,1-AUC)"]:.2f}, so it was never used. Survey year, region, the year-region code and all identifiers were also excluded as predictors. Ten 2012 households are labelled Afar but have coordinates south of 8 degrees N; region is not a predictor, and the records were kept as published.')
add('h2', '3.3 Feature engineering')
para(f'Five nested feature sets were built ({T("fs")}). FS1 contains the five environmental variables. FS2 adds six climate indices, FS3 adds four coordinate terms, FS4 adds 18 neighbourhood and cross-disease rates, and FS5 adds six sampling-density terms, giving 39 features. The rates are computed for all three diseases, so the FMD model also sees nearby CBPP and LSD rates (cross-disease rates). Each set adds one group to the previous set, so the difference between consecutive sets estimates what that group adds, given the groups before it.')
table('fs', 'Nested feature sets used in the ablation', list(T2.columns), T2.values.tolist(), [700, 1900, 4300, 650, 650, 2200], size=14)
para('Neighbourhood rates use only the training households of the current split. For a query location x, the kernel rate for disease d at bandwidth b is given by Eq. (1).')
add('equation', 'r_{b,d}(x) = [ Σ_{j} K_{b}(x, j) · pos_{d}(j) + c · p_{0,d}(x) ] / [ Σ_{j} K_{b}(x, j) · n(j) + c ],   K_{b}(x, j) = exp( −dist(x, j)^{2} / (2b^{2}) )', number=1)
para('Here j runs over training locations outside the 81 km block that contains x, n(j) and pos_{d}(j) are the numbers of households and positive households at j, c = 1 is a prior weight, and p_{0,d}(x) is the training rate outside the block of x. Bandwidths were 50, 150 and 400 km. The k-nearest-location rates replace the kernel sum with the k nearest training locations outside the block (k = 5, 15 and 40).')
para('We call this construction leakage-safe because it follows three rules, each of which was tested (Section 4.3). (i) Rates are fitted on the training rows of the split only. (ii) The whole block of the query location is excluded, for training rows and test rows alike, so a household never uses its own label or the labels of its village, and training features are built under the same conditions as test features. (iii) Neighbours are ordered by distance rounded to 10^{-6} km and then by location ID, because coarse coordinates create many exact ties; the result therefore does not depend on the order of the input rows.')
para('These rules were tested in five ways (Section 4.3): (1) an independent loop-based reimplementation of every outcome-derived feature; (2) flipping all outer-test labels, which must leave features, predictions, calibration and thresholds unchanged; (3) flipping single training labels, which must not change the features of the household\'s own block; (4) checking that all inner folds are block-grouped and contain no outer-test household; and (5) negative controls that rebuild the whole pipeline on labels permuted between households or between whole villages. Passing these checks shows that the features do not use test labels. It does not show that they improve prediction; that question is answered by the ablation.')
add('h2', '3.4 Machine-learning models')
para(f'The feature-set comparison and the ablation used ExtraTrees {{Geurts2006}} with 300 trees, a minimum leaf size of 10 and the square root of the number of features at each split. The ensemble was the equal-weight rank average of 23 models ({T("models")}): generalised additive models, random forests {{Breiman2001}}, XGBoost {{Chen2016}}, CatBoost {{Prokhorenkova2018}}, LightGBM {{Ke2017}}, logistic regression, k-nearest neighbours, a multilayer perceptron, a support vector machine and a location-level Gaussian-process (kriging) model, fitted with scikit-learn {{Pedregosa2011}} and the respective libraries. All hyperparameters were fixed in advance, and the values in {T("models")} were read from the model objects in the code.')
para(f'For each library model, the test scores were converted to their empirical-distribution ranks within that model\'s inner out-of-fold scores, and the 23 ranks were averaged ((a) rank average). Because only ranks are averaged, models with different output scales, such as the support vector machine decision function, can be combined. As a comparator, (b) is the single library model with the best inner out-of-fold ROC-AUC in each split. One library member, a group-balanced CatBoost model, weights each training household by the inverse size of its survey year-region group. The year-region code is therefore used as a training weight, although never as a predictor; the weights use training rows only and do not change when labels are permuted. Removing this model changed the mean rank-average ROC-AUC by at most {ngb_max:.3f} (largest change in a single fold {ngb_fold:.3f}; Supplementary Table S21).')
para('Two corrections were made after the leakage audit (Section 4.3): the k-nearest tie rule, and the exclusion of the own block from the fallback prior of training rows in the library code. All results in this paper use the corrected code, except the confirmatory test, which is reported as observed.')
table('models', 'Models and fixed hyperparameters (read from the fitted objects)', list(T3.columns), T3.values.tolist(), [3000, 2000, 4700], size=14,
      note='Library feature sets: B = 5 environmental variables; E = environmental, climate and coordinate terms (14); EL = E plus kernel rates and densities (26); ELK = EL plus k-nearest rates and distance (36); G = location-level inputs for kriging. The number of library members is given in brackets (23 in total).')
add('h2', '3.5 Spatial validation')
para(f'Households were assigned to square blocks of 81 km. Blocks were allocated to five folds with balanced household counts ({D1["D1"]["fold_households_range"][0]}-{D1["D1"]["fold_households_range"][1]} test households per fold), and the procedure was repeated three times with different grid origins, giving 15 development splits. No location or block appeared in both the training and test sets of any split. Within each training set, five inner block-grouped folds were used for calibration, thresholds and ensemble ranking. Random 5-fold cross-validation, at household level and with the same corrected code, was run as a reference.')
para(f'The block size was set in an earlier analysis as the median of the practical ranges of three outcome variograms ({v_out.iloc[0]:.0f}, {v_out.iloc[1]:.0f} and {v_out.iloc[2]:.0f} km for CBPP, FMD and LSD). These variograms used all labels, so the evaluation design, though not any model, depended on outcome data. Variograms of the five environmental variables, which use no labels, had practical ranges of {v_free.min():.0f} km to beyond 600 km (Supplementary Table S12). We therefore report 120 km and 160 km blocks as a sensitivity analysis, together with proximity buffers that remove training locations within 25, 50 or 100 km of any test location, and size-matched random removal as a control.')
para('To describe how new the test data were, we computed for every test household the distance to the nearest training location. We also computed the area of applicability (AOA) of the FS1 and FS5 ExtraTrees models {Meyer2021}. The dissimilarity index (DI) of a test household is its distance to the nearest training household in standardised predictor space, with each predictor weighted by its importance in the fitted model, divided by the mean distance between training households. A household lies outside the AOA when its DI exceeds the upper whisker (third quartile plus 1.5 times the interquartile range) of the DI values of training households computed across inner folds. No gridded predictor surfaces were available, so the AOA was computed at surveyed locations only. The DI measures how unusual the predictor values are; it is not a measure of disease risk.')
para('A separate confirmatory partition (2 × 5 folds, with different grid origins) was generated and sealed before any model was evaluated. It was first reserved for an earlier, pre-registered study of automated feature and model selection by population-based optimisation. That method did not pass its own go/no-go criterion on the development folds for any disease, so it was never tested on the partition. The partition was then assigned to two hypotheses about FS5: H1, FS5 has a higher ROC-AUC than FS1 for both FMD and LSD; and H2, FS5 and the rank average differ. These hypotheses were recorded before the partition was opened but after the development results were known. The confirmatory test is therefore a held-out check of hypotheses suggested by the development data, not an independent pre-registered confirmation. The partition was opened once, with the code in use at that time (before the two corrections in Section 3.4). The optimisation study is outside the scope of this paper; it is reported in Supplementary Section S2, and none of its results are used here.')
add('h2', '3.6 Statistical evaluation')
para('The primary metric was ROC-AUC. We also report PR-AUC with its no-skill level (the prevalence) {Saito2015}, the Brier score, calibration intercept and slope {VanCalster2019}, and sensitivity, specificity, balanced accuracy, F1 and the Matthews correlation coefficient {Chicco2020}. Probabilities came from Platt (logistic) calibration fitted on inner out-of-fold scores; if the inner association was negative, the output was set to the training prevalence. Thresholds were chosen by the Youden index on the same inner data. Calibration was assessed in two ways: within each test fold (fold-level medians) and on the pooled out-of-fold predictions with location-cluster bootstrap intervals. The Brier skill score compares the Brier score with that of a constant forecast equal to the prevalence, p(1 - p); values at or below zero mean no skill.')
para('Survey-campaign confounding was measured in three ways. First, the within-year ROC-AUC of Eq. (2), where AUC_{t} is the ROC-AUC among households from survey year t, and P_{t} and N_{t} are the numbers of positive and negative households in that year. Only pairs from the same year are compared, so differences in prevalence between campaigns cannot raise the score.')
add('equation', 'AUC_{within} = Σ_{t} P_{t} · N_{t} · AUC_{t} / Σ_{t} P_{t} · N_{t}', number=2)
para('Second, survey-year identification: under the same spatial splits, an ExtraTrees classifier predicted the survey year of each test household from FS1, from the 18 neighbourhood rates alone, or from FS5, and was scored by the macro one-versus-rest ROC-AUC. A high score means that the features carry information about the campaign. Third, campaign-specific models: in every development split and for each survey year, FS1 and FS5 ExtraTrees were fitted on that year\'s training households only and tested on that year\'s test households, and compared with the pooled models on the same households, using test sets with at least five positive and five negative households.')
para(f'Paired differences were tested over the 15 development folds with the corrected resampled t-test {{Nadeau2003}}. Its variance correction uses the ratio of test to training households, which was 668/2672 = 1/4 for the 81 km design; for the proximity-buffer and block-size conditions, the mean test and training sizes of each condition were used. As a second method, a location-cluster bootstrap (1,000 resamples) was applied to the pooled out-of-fold predictions of each repeat, with scores expressed as quantiles of the training out-of-fold distribution. Holm correction {{Holm1979}} was applied across the three diseases, across the 12 ablation steps and across the six permutation tests. Negative controls rebuilt the whole pipeline, including the neighbourhood features, on permuted labels for development repeat 1: {n_perm} household-level permutations and {n_perm} village-level permutations that move whole villages between locations of similar size. The permutation p-value is (1 + number of null gains at least as large as the observed gain) / (1 + number of permutations), so with {n_perm} permutations it cannot be smaller than {1 / (n_perm + 1):.4f}. Forward temporal validation trained on 2012-2015 households (n = 1,564) and tested on 2018-2019 households (n = 1,776).')
para('For explainability, exact SHAP values {Lundberg2017, Lundberg2020} of the FS5 ExtraTrees model were computed for the test households of the five folds of development repeat 1, and summarised as the mean absolute SHAP value per feature and per feature group. Partial dependence {Friedman2001} was computed on the training data of each fold, and grouped permutation importance (the drop in test ROC-AUC when a feature group is permuted) on all 15 folds. Analyses used Python 3.13, scikit-learn 1.9.1, XGBoost 3.4.1, LightGBM 4.7.0, CatBoost 1.2.10 and SHAP 0.54.1 with fixed seeds; an executable notebook reproduces every table and figure. The workflow is summarised in ' + F('workflow') + '.')
figure('workflow', F2D / 'Fig1_workflow_v7.png', 'Study workflow. Steps in blue boxes are repeated inside the training data of each split.', width=560)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4 results
add('h1', '4. Results and discussion')
add('h2', '4.1 Spatial generalisation')
para(f'Random 5-fold cross-validation gave mean fold ROC-AUC of {min(rcv_all):.2f}-{max(rcv_all):.2f} for every model and disease ({T("perf")}, {F("randspat")}). Spatial block validation, run with the same code, gave {min(fm(d, m) for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f}-{max(fm(d, m) for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f}. For the rank average, random splits raised the mean fold ROC-AUC by {gap_a["CBPP"]:.3f} (CBPP), {gap_a["FMD"]:.3f} (FMD) and {gap_a["LSD"]:.3f} (LSD); across all four models the difference was {min(gap_all):.2f}-{max(gap_all):.2f}. The reason is clear from the distances: under random splits, {same_loc_random:.1f}% of test households had training households at the same location, whereas under 81 km blocks the median distance to the nearest training location was {DST.loc["Spatial 81 km", "Median (km)"]:.0f} km (interquartile range {DST.loc["Spatial 81 km", "IQR (km)"].replace("-", " to ")} km, over all test households; Supplementary Table S22 and Figure S9). Random splits also raised within-year ROC-AUC, from {min(fm(d, m, "within_year_AUC") for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f}-{max(fm(d, m, "within_year_AUC") for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f} to {min(rc(d, m, "within_year_AUC") for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f}-{max(rc(d, m, "within_year_AUC") for d in DIS for m in MODELS):.2f}, because households from the same village appear in both training and test sets.')
