hf = t10('household', 'FMD')
vl = t10('village', 'LSD'); hl = t10('household', 'LSD')
para(f'Under permuted labels, ROC-AUC stayed close to 0.5 and the FS5 gain stayed close to zero ({T("null")}; Supplementary Figure S6). The permutations used development repeat 1 only, so the observed and null values are means over its five folds; for FMD the observed gain was {float(hf["Observed gain"]):.3f} (against {fmd_gain:.3f} over all 15 folds). This gain was larger than all {n_perm} household-level and all {n_perm} village-level permutations, giving p = {p_fmd[0]} in both designs, the smallest value that {n_perm} permutations can give. After Holm correction across the six tests (three diseases × two designs), p = {float(hf["Holm p (6 tests)"]):.3f}, slightly above 0.05. The FMD gain is therefore unlikely to arise from the feature construction or from chance alone, but this test does not establish it at the 5% level after correction. With 1,000 permutations per design, the smallest attainable Holm-adjusted p-value would be {6 / 1001:.3f}, so more permutations would give a more decisive test. The LSD gain ({float(hl["Observed gain"]):.3f}) exceeded the household-level null (p = {hl["Permutation p (resolution)"].split(" ")[0]}) but not the village-level null (p = {vl["Permutation p (resolution)"].split(" ")[0]}), which keeps villages intact, so it can be explained by clustering within villages. CBPP did not differ from either null. The leakage checks found no path by which test labels reached the models; the remaining design dependence is the block size, which was derived from labels (Section 3.5) and is examined in Section 4.5.')
