table('null', f'Negative controls: full pipeline rebuilt on permuted labels ({n_perm} permutations per scheme, development repeat 1; observed and null values are means over its five folds)', ['Null scheme', 'Disease', 'Null FS5 ROC-AUC [2.5-97.5%]', 'Null FS5 - FS1 [2.5-97.5%]', 'Observed FS5 - FS1', 'Permutation p (resolution)', 'Holm p'], rows,
      [1100, 800, 1900, 1900, 1200, 1500, 900], note=f'Permutation p = (1 + null gains at least as large as the observed gain) / (1 + {n_perm}); the smallest attainable value is {1 / (n_perm + 1):.4f}. Holm correction across the six tests (three diseases × two schemes).')

add('h2', '4.4 Model explainability')
top = lambda d, g: sg(d, g)
para(f'SHAP values show what the FS5 model uses ({F("shap")}). For CBPP, the top four features were own-disease k-nearest and kernel rates, followed by altitude and precipitation. For FMD, the most important features were the 400 km CBPP kernel rate, latitude and latitude plus longitude, followed by FMD rates at short range. For LSD, LSD and FMD rates at several ranges ranked highest. Summed over groups, own-disease or cross-disease rates had the largest mean absolute SHAP value for all three diseases (FMD: own-disease {top("FMD", "Own-disease rates"):.3f}, cross-disease {top("FMD", "Cross-disease rates"):.3f}, coordinates {top("FMD", "Coordinates"):.3f}, climate indices {top("FMD", "Climate indices"):.3f}; Supplementary Table S17). The group ranking agreed with grouped permutation importance (Spearman correlation {min(rho_shap.values()):.2f}-{max(rho_shap.values()):.2f} over the six groups).')
para('These explanations need care. The FS5 model relies heavily on the rates, but the ablation shows that removing them after the coordinates costs little for FMD. Rates and coordinates both describe location, so the fitted model can use either; SHAP shares the credit between correlated features, and it shows reliance, not necessity. The broad 400 km rates in particular describe regional prevalence, which differs between survey campaigns. Partial dependence (Supplementary Figure S8) supports this reading: for LSD, the predicted probability fell as the regional 400 km LSD rate rose, a pattern that is hard to explain as a local disease process and easier to explain as a contrast between campaigns. Feature importance in this study therefore describes the models, not risk factors, and the cross-disease rates should not be read as evidence of a biological link between diseases.')
figure('shap', F2D / 'R2Fig2_shap_top_features_v1.png', 'Ten features with the largest mean absolute SHAP value for the FS5 ExtraTrees model (test households of the five folds of development repeat 1), coloured by feature group.')

add('h2', '4.5 Spatial applicability')
