add('h2', '4.6 Survey-campaign confounding')
camp = D5.set_index('Survey year')
para(f'Reported prevalence changed sharply between survey campaigns ({T("data")}, {F("years")}). CBPP was reported by {yprev["CBPP"][2014]:.1f}% of households in 2014 but {yprev["CBPP"][2019]:.1f}% in 2019, and LSD rose from {yprev["LSD"][2018]:.1f}% in 2018 to {yprev["LSD"][2019]:.1f}% in 2019. The campaigns overlapped only partly in space. Between {camp["Locations with another campaign within 81 km (%)"].min():.0f}% and {camp["Locations with another campaign within 81 km (%)"].max():.0f}% of a campaign\'s locations had a location of another campaign within 81 km, but the regions surveyed differed: Amhara, Benishangul-Gumuz, Gambela and Tigray were surveyed only in 2018-2019 (Supplementary Table S13). In a one-way analysis, survey year explained {100 * eta_year["CBPP"]:.1f}%, {100 * eta_year["FMD"]:.1f}% and {100 * eta_year["LSD"]:.1f}% of the household-level outcome variance for CBPP, FMD and LSD.')
para(f'The features identified the survey campaign ({T("cid")}). Under spatial validation, the survey year of a test household was predicted with a macro ROC-AUC of {cid_m["FS1"][0]:.3f} from the five environmental variables, {cid_m["Neighbourhood rates only"][0]:.3f} from the 18 neighbourhood rates alone and {cid_m["FS5"][0]:.3f} from FS5 (chance level 0.5). The rates identified the year at least as well as the environmental variables (difference {rates_vs_fs1[0]:+.3f}, 95% CI {rates_vs_fs1[1]:+.3f} to {rates_vs_fs1[2]:+.3f}). Location therefore carries information about the campaign, and the campaign carries information about the outcome. These campaign-related signals reflect when and where the surveys were carried out; they are not evidence of biological relationships between the diseases or of risk factors.')
