wc, wf, wl = bs('CBPP', '(a) Rank average', 'within_year'), bs('FMD', 'FS5', 'within_year'), bs('LSD', '(a) Rank average', 'within_year')
para(f'Within-year ROC-AUC was much lower than overall ROC-AUC ({T("perf")}, {F("wy")}). For the best overall model of each disease (the rank average for CBPP and LSD, FS5 for FMD), it was {wc[0]:.3f} for CBPP, {wf[0]:.3f} for FMD and {wl[0]:.3f} for LSD, against overall pooled values of {bs("CBPP", "(a) Rank average")[0]:.3f}, {bs("FMD", "FS5")[0]:.3f} and {bs("LSD", "(a) Rank average")[0]:.3f}. A large part of the overall discrimination therefore comes from separating campaigns with high and low reported prevalence. Part of the drop may also arise because each campaign covers a smaller area with less environmental contrast; the two explanations cannot be fully separated with these data.')
figure('wy', FIG / 'Fig3_overall_vs_within_year_AUC_v1.png', 'Overall (filled) and within-survey-year (open) pooled ROC-AUC with 95% location-cluster bootstrap intervals. (a) rank average; (b) inner-selected single model. The dotted line marks chance level.')
rw = t7('FS5 minus FS1', 'FMD'); rlw = t7('FS5 minus FS1', 'LSD'); ya = YA[(YA.disease == 'FMD') & (YA.model == 'FS5')].set_index('year').AUC
para(f'The FMD feature gain persisted within years: {rw["Within-year: pooled [bootstrap 95% CI]"]} by bootstrap, positive in {rw["Within-year: folds > 0"]} folds. The corrected t-interval included zero ({rw["Within-year: fold mean [corrected-t 95% CI]"]}), so this evidence is supportive rather than conclusive. For LSD, the overall gain of FS5 reversed within years ({rlw["Within-year: pooled [bootstrap 95% CI]"]}), which suggests that it mainly reflected campaign differences. Year-specific ROC-AUC varied widely; for FMD with FS5 it ranged from {ya.min():.2f} to {ya.max():.2f} (Supplementary Table S2).')
c_l5, c_c5 = rt2('LSD', 'FS5'), rt2('CBPP', 'FS5')
para(f'Models trained on a single campaign ranked households within that campaign about as well as the pooled models ({T("camp")}, {F("camp")}). None of the six differences was clearly different from zero. With FS5, the campaign-specific models tended to do better for LSD ({ci_flat(c_l5["Campaign-specific minus pooled [corrected-t 95% CI]"])}; {c_l5["Folds favouring campaign-specific"]} folds) and CBPP ({ci_flat(c_c5["Campaign-specific minus pooled [corrected-t 95% CI]"])}; {c_c5["Folds favouring campaign-specific"]} folds), and equally well for FMD. Pooling campaigns therefore did not improve the ranking of households within a campaign, and for LSD the pooled neighbourhood rates, dominated by other campaigns\' prevalence, may have made it worse. Within a campaign, location still explained {100 * eta_loc_within.min():.0f}-{100 * eta_loc_within.max():.0f}% of the remaining outcome variance, so there is real spatial structure within campaigns that the models only partly capture.')
table('camp', 'Within-campaign ROC-AUC of models trained on all campaigns (pooled) and on one campaign at a time (development, 15 splits, ExtraTrees)', list(RT2.columns), [[f'{v:.3f}' if isinstance(v, float) else v for v in row] for row in RT2.values.tolist()],
      [800, 800, 1000, 1500, 1600, 2300, 1300], note='Fold-level ROC-AUC within each survey year, weighted by positive × negative pairs and averaged over the 15 folds; only year-fold test sets with at least five positive and five negative households. 95% CI: corrected resampled t.')
figure('camp', RF / 'Fig6_campaign_specific_vs_pooled_v3.png', 'Difference in within-campaign ROC-AUC between campaign-specific and pooled models, with corrected resampled t 95% intervals; the numbers on the right give the pooled and campaign-specific values.', width=520)
para('These findings are consistent with the choice of Gizaw et al. to model survey year and location jointly {Gizaw2026preprint}, and with the view of Moreno-Torres et al. that a change between the training and test populations is a separate source of error {MorenoTorres2012}. Studies of pooled survey data should report within-campaign discrimination next to the overall score.')

add('h2', '4.7 Temporal generalisation')
tp = {d: (100 * T12[T12.Disease == d]['Train prevalence'].iloc[0], 100 * T12[T12.Disease == d]['Test prevalence'].iloc[0]) for d in DIS}
para(f'Models trained on the 2012-2015 campaigns and tested on 2018-2019 did not show reliable discrimination ({T("temp")}, {F("temp")}). ROC-AUC was below 0.5 for all three diseases and both feature sets ({min(temp_vals):.2f}-{max(temp_vals):.2f}), and the 95% interval lay entirely below 0.5 in {n_temp_below} of the 6 cases. Prevalence also shifted: CBPP fell from {tp["CBPP"][0]:.1f}% in training to {tp["CBPP"][1]:.1f}% in testing, FMD from {tp["FMD"][0]:.1f}% to {tp["FMD"][1]:.1f}%, and LSD from {tp["LSD"][0]:.1f}% to {tp["LSD"][1]:.1f}%.')
para('Several things changed at once between the two periods. Reported prevalence fell for CBPP and FMD, the later campaigns surveyed regions that the earlier campaigns had not covered (Amhara, Benishangul-Gumuz, Gambela and Tigray), the projects may have differed in sampling and questionnaire conditions, and outbreaks themselves vary over time. Because place and time changed together, this test cannot separate their effects. ROC-AUC values below 0.5 mean that the households ranked as higher risk by models built on the early surveys reported disease less often in the later surveys. This is the difference between retrospective mapping and forecasting described in Section 1: the spatial results describe how well reported occurrence can be interpolated within the surveyed period, not how well future outbreaks can be predicted. Transferability across time is a known weak point of predictive spatial models {Yates2018}. The models in this study should not be used to forecast where outbreaks will occur.')
table('temp', 'Forward temporal validation (train 2012-2015, n = 1,564; test 2018-2019, n = 1,776)', ['Disease', 'Features', 'Train prevalence (%)', 'Test prevalence (%)', 'ROC-AUC [95% CI]', 'PR-AUC (no-skill)'],
      [[r.Disease, r.Features, f'{100 * r["Train prevalence"]:.1f}', f'{100 * r["Test prevalence"]:.1f}', r['ROC-AUC [95% CI]'], r['PR-AUC (no-skill)']] for _, r in T12.iterrows()],
      [1100, 1000, 1500, 1500, 2400, 2100], note='95% CI from 1,000 location-cluster bootstrap resamples of the test set.')
figure('temp', RF / 'Fig8_forward_temporal_v1.png', '(a) Prevalence in the 2012-2015 training campaigns and the 2018-2019 test campaigns. (b) Forward temporal ROC-AUC with 95% location-cluster bootstrap intervals; the dotted line marks chance level.')
lsd_a = T4[(T4.Disease == 'LSD') & (T4.Model == '(a) Rank average')].iloc[0]['ROC-AUC pooled [95% CI]']; lsd_wy = T5[(T5.Disease == 'LSD') & (T5.Model == '(a) Rank average')].iloc[0]['Within-year AUC pooled [95% CI]']
para(f'The evidence for LSD is the weakest of the three diseases. Under spatial validation, the pooled ROC-AUC of the rank average was {ci_txt(lsd_a)}, but within single survey years it was {ci_txt(lsd_wy)}. FS5 did not improve LSD within years ({ci_flat(t7("FS5 minus FS1", "LSD")["Within-year: pooled [bootstrap 95% CI]"]).replace(", 95% CI", ", bootstrap 95% CI")}), its small overall gain did not exceed the village-level permutation null, and the gain on the confirmatory partition was {S9.loc["LSD", "H1 FS5 - FS1: pooled"]:+.3f}. The LSD probabilities had no skill beyond the prevalence (Brier skill score {z2(bss_rng("LSD")[0])} to {z2(bss_rng("LSD")[1])}), and forward temporal ROC-AUC was {temp[("LSD", "FS1")].split(" ")[0]} with FS1 and {temp[("LSD", "FS5")].split(" ")[0]} with FS5. LSD maps from these data should therefore be read as a description of where LSD was reported during the surveyed period, not as reliable risk estimates or as a basis for prediction.')

add('h2', '4.8 Comparison with previous studies')
para(f'High accuracy for livestock disease occurrence has been reported with random splits {{AfshariSafavi2022}}. Our random-split scores were also high ({min(rcv_all):.2f}-{max(rcv_all):.2f}), but spatial validation lowered them by {min(gap_all):.2f}-{max(gap_all):.2f}, and within-year analysis lowered them further. This agrees with findings in ecological mapping {{Ploton2020, Roberts2017}}.')
para('Gizaw et al. checked their kriged maps of the same data by 10-fold cross-validation and reported correlations above 0.99 between observed and predicted values {Gizaw2026preprint}. Because the kriging was applied to model-fitted values, this check measures how well the map interpolates a fitted risk surface. Our question is different: how well household-level occurrence can be ranked in areas without survey data. The two analyses agree that survey year and location are entangled, and we show how much this inflates the apparent discrimination of machine-learning models.')

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5 limitations, 6 conclusion
add('h1', '5. Limitations')
for t in ['Outcomes are household-reported occurrence over a recall period. They can include recall bias, misclassification and under-reporting, and they are not laboratory confirmed.',
          'The surveys used purposive and stratified cluster sampling within project areas {Gizaw2026preprint}. They are not a probability sample of Ethiopia, so the results describe the surveyed areas only. The area of applicability was computed at surveyed locations only, because no gridded predictor surfaces were available.',
          'Survey year and region could not be separated from location. Within-year ROC-AUC removes between-year differences in prevalence, but campaigns also differed in area, questionnaire and season. Some years have very few cases (two CBPP cases in 2019).',
          'Survey years are pooled in the neighbourhood features: on average 31% of the 150 km kernel weight comes from later surveys. This is acceptable for retrospective mapping but not for prospective use.',
          'The ablation added feature groups in one fixed order, so each step measures what a group adds given the groups before it; other orders were not tested. SHAP and permutation importance were computed on one development repeat and share credit between correlated features.',
          'The 81 km block size was derived from outcome variograms that used all labels, and label-free variograms suggest longer ranges. Block-size sensitivity was explored after the confirmatory partition had been opened, so it is exploratory. Coordinates are given to 0.1 degree, so proximity analyses below about 11 km are not meaningful.',
          f'The confirmatory hypotheses were written after the development results were known, so the confirmatory test is a held-out check, not an independent pre-registered confirmation. It also used the code before the two corrections; on the development splits the corrections changed mean ensemble ROC-AUC by at most {prior_max:.4f}. The ablation, explainability, applicability and campaign-identification analyses were added after the confirmatory partition had been opened and used the development splits only. The permutation tests used {n_perm} permutations per scheme, which limits p-values to {1 / (n_perm + 1):.3f} or above and the Holm-adjusted FMD p-value to {6 / (n_perm + 1):.3f}; more permutations would be needed for a stronger test.',
          'The study shows association, not causation. Feature importance does not identify risk factors.']:
    bullet(t)
add('h1', '6. Conclusion')
para(f'We evaluated machine-learning models for retrospective mapping of household-reported CBPP, FMD and LSD in Ethiopia with spatial block validation, a leakage audit and several sensitivity analyses. Random cross-validation, run with the same code, gave mean fold ROC-AUC {min(gap_a.values()):.2f}-{max(gap_a.values()):.2f} higher than spatial validation for the ensemble, because almost every random test household had training households at the same location.')
para(f'The engineered feature set improved FMD prediction by about {fmd_gain:.2f} ROC-AUC. The gain was larger than all label permutations in two null designs, although the Holm-adjusted permutation p-value ({holm_fmd:.3f}) was slightly above 0.05. It was also positive on a held-out partition whose hypotheses were fixed after the development results, and it persisted under proximity removal and larger blocks. The ablation shows that the gain comes mainly from climate indices and coordinates, while neighbourhood and cross-disease rates add little once location is known and partly encode the survey campaign. The leakage audit shows that these rates are safe, not that they are needed. The same features gave no consistent benefit for CBPP or LSD. For LSD, the probabilities had no skill beyond the prevalence, and discrimination within survey years and forward in time was close to or below chance.')
para(f'Survey campaigns confound pooled spatial accuracy. Survey year explained {100 * eta_year.min():.1f}-{100 * eta_year.max():.1f}% of outcome variance and could be predicted from the features, discrimination within a single survey year was modest (about {min(wy_best.values()):.2f}-{max(wy_best.values()):.2f}), training on all campaigns did not improve within-campaign ranking, and the models did not transfer forward in time. Test households were inside the area of applicability, so the loss of accuracy is more likely to reflect differences in reported prevalence than unusual predictor values.')
para('Within a single campaign, the maps can help rank surveyed areas for follow-up surveillance, but they should not be used to compare risk across campaigns or years, or to forecast. Studies that pool campaign-based surveys should report within-campaign discrimination next to overall scores, test outcome-derived features for leakage and with an ablation, and use probability-based or repeated surveys if the aim is forecasting.')

